# DevOps / SRE / Web Architecture Assignment

## Section 1 — DevOps: Why? (8 pts)

### 1.1 The problem DevOps was invented to solve (3 pts)

Historically, the people who wrote software and the people who ran it in production were separate teams with conflicting incentives: developers were rewarded for shipping new features fast, while operations was rewarded for keeping systems stable, so operations resisted change and developers threw code "over the wall" without owning what happened to it in production. This split created long handoff delays, finger-pointing when things broke, and infrequent, high-risk releases because nobody had end-to-end responsibility for getting working software into users' hands quickly and safely. DevOps was invented to close that gap by giving teams shared tools, automation, and accountability across the whole software lifecycle, from writing code to running it live.

### 1.2 Quarterly, weekend-long releases with a 33% rollback rate (3 pts)

**(a) Business problems:**
1. Slow time-to-market — features and fixes sit unreleased for up to 4 months, so the company can't respond to customers or competitors quickly.
2. Revenue/reputation risk — a 1-in-3 rollback rate means customers regularly hit broken functionality or outages right after release, damaging trust.
3. High opportunity cost — deployments require dedicating a whole weekend (often with overtime pay or on-call costs), which is expensive and hard to schedule around other business needs.

**(b) Engineering problems:**
1. Burnout — weekend, high-pressure release windows are exhausting and unsustainable, especially when they recur every few months.
2. Large, risky batches — 4 months of changes get bundled into one release, making it very hard to isolate which change caused a failure when a rollback happens.
3. Atrophied release skills — because releases are rare, the process stays manual and error-prone; the team never gets enough repetitions to build confidence, tooling, or muscle memory around deploying safely.

### 1.3 True/False (2 pts)

| # | Statement | T/F | Why |
|---|-----------|-----|-----|
| a | DevOps means "developers do operations work too." | False | DevOps is about shared ownership and collaboration across the lifecycle, not literally merging job functions into one person's task list. |
| b | If you have a CI/CD pipeline, you are doing DevOps. | False | CI/CD is a tool/practice that supports DevOps, but DevOps also requires the collaboration, feedback loops, and shared responsibility around that pipeline — a pipeline alone doesn't guarantee those exist. |
| c | DevOps aims to make deployments boring. | True | The goal is frequent, small, low-risk, automated, predictable releases — "boring" in the sense of routine and low-drama rather than rare, high-stakes events. |
| d | DevOps is a job title. | False | DevOps is a set of practices/principles; "DevOps Engineer" as a title exists in industry, but it's a loose label for someone working on these practices, not what DevOps itself refers to. |

