## Section 4 — CSR vs SSR (10 pts)

### 4.1 Table (4 pts)

| | CSR | SSR |
|---|---|---|
| First paint speed on slow phone | slow — blank page until JS downloads/parses/executes |  fast — HTML arrives already rendered |
| SEO out of the box |  crawlers may see an empty shell unless JS is executed |  full content is in the initial HTML |
| Server CPU cost |  low — server just serves static JS/assets |  higher — server renders HTML per request |
| Works with a static host (no server) |  yes — just static files |  no — needs a running server/runtime |

### 4.2 Product choices (3 pts)

a) **News site on Google traffic → SSR** (or SSG) — SEO and fast first paint matter enormously for organic search traffic and indexing.

b) **Internal admin dashboard behind login → CSR** — no SEO need, content is private, and interactivity/dev speed matter more than initial paint.

c) **Marketing landing page with contact form → SSR/SSG** — it needs to be indexable and load instantly for conversion, with the form as a small interactive island.

### 4.3 Where SSG fits (2 pts)

SSG sits between CSR and SSR: like SSR, it produces fully-formed HTML that's fast to paint and good for SEO, but instead of rendering on every request it renders once at build time and serves the result as static files — like CSR's static-host deployment model. This gives you SSR's speed/SEO benefits with CSR's near-zero server cost and easy hosting, at the cost of the content being only as fresh as the last build (not suited to highly dynamic, per-user, or frequently changing content).

