## Section 5 — Cloudflare & Cloudflare Pages (10 pts)

### 5.1 What a CDN does (2 pts)

A CDN caches copies of content on servers ("edge nodes") distributed around the world, so users get content from a nearby server instead of the origin. Example: an image is hosted on a server in Virginia; without a CDN, a user in Yerevan's request has to travel across the Atlantic and back (high latency, ~150–200ms+ round trip). With a CDN, the first Yerevan request pulls the image from Virginia and caches it at a nearby edge node (e.g. in Europe or the Caucasus region); every subsequent request from users near Yerevan is served from that nearby cache, cutting latency dramatically and reducing load on the Virginia origin server.

### 5.2 Match features (3 pts)

| Feature | What it does |
|---|---|
| DNS | Resolves domain names to IP addresses; Cloudflare hosts and serves your DNS records |
| Proxy (orange cloud) | Routes traffic through Cloudflare's network, hiding your origin IP and enabling other features (WAF, cache, etc.) |
| WAF | Filters/blocks malicious HTTP requests (SQLi, XSS, bad bots) before they reach your server |
| Cloudflare Pages | Hosts and deploys static/JAMstack sites directly from a git repo, with builds and previews |
| Cloudflare Workers | Runs serverless JavaScript/WASM code at the edge, close to users |
| Cache | Stores copies of content at edge locations so repeat requests don't hit your origin server |

### 5.3 Orange vs grey cloud (2 pts)

Orange cloud (proxied) means traffic to that DNS record routes through Cloudflare's network, getting caching, WAF, DDoS protection, and IP-hiding. Grey cloud (DNS only) means Cloudflare just resolves the domain to an IP, but traffic goes directly to your origin server with none of Cloudflare's protection/caching applied.

You'd deliberately choose grey cloud for something like a **mail server record (e.g. an MX-related A record, or an SSH/admin subdomain)**, where you need direct protocol-level access (not HTTP) or must expose the real origin IP for the service to work correctly.

### 5.4 Cloudflare Pages vs $5 VPS + Nginx (2 pts)

**Cloudflare Pages advantages:**
1. Free global CDN distribution and automatic HTTPS, with zero server maintenance (no patching, no uptime babysitting).
2. Built-in CI/CD from git — push to deploy, plus automatic preview deployments per branch/PR.

**VPS + Nginx advantages:**
1. Full control — you can run any backend, cron jobs, or custom server-side logic, not just static files.
2. No vendor platform limits (build minutes, request quotas, feature gating) — you configure Nginx however you want.

### 5.5 Preview deployment (1 pt)

A Cloudflare Pages preview deployment is an automatically generated, unique URL for each branch/pull-request build, separate from production. It's useful in a team because reviewers, QA, and stakeholders can see and test a change live before it's merged and deployed to production, catching issues early without affecting real users.
